<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<!--
  English source strings.

  Two groups, kept apart on purpose:

    1. UI strings, referenced through R.string by the Compose code.
    2. Catalog strings, referenced by key at runtime from :core. Their names are generated by
       `./gradlew :core:dumpCatalogManifest`, and MainActivity audits them on every debug launch, so a
       scenario option added without a translation is caught in the build rather than by a worker
       looking at a blank button.

  Wording rules that are load-bearing rather than stylistic:

    * A queued upload is never described as an error. The training is already recorded and signed; the
      queue is a delivery detail, and calling it a failure teaches workers to distrust a working system.
    * "Tamper-evident hash chain", never "blockchain". It is a hash chain, and overselling it would be
      the first thing an assessor picked apart.
    * Instructions are imperative and short. A worker reads these in a headlamp beam, through a
      scratched screen protector, wearing gloves.
-->
